From eabbcde5ea583833cdf593667206cefb3f40de4d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 21:03:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: another Dolby Vision output for the video opens
 its video stream alone, the sound playing on

A choice in "Dolby Vision output (this video)" opened the video stream
that plays again with an accurate seek of the whole player: the sound
stopped for as long as a seek takes. Now only the video starts again
(VideoOnlyReopen.h, CVideoPlayer::ReopenVideoOnly()):

- the demuxer goes back alone to the key frame before the clock (the
  target computed as a seek of the player computes it);
- the video stream is closed and opened, and its packets before the clock
  and half a second are decoded, not shown, as after an accurate seek
  (CheckPlayerInit()); where its decoder cannot start at that key frame,
  it starts at the next one, as after any seek;
- the packets of the other streams that the demuxer reads again, up to
  the last one each player was sent (CCurrentStream::dts), are thrown
  away before anything looks at them (CRequeueFilter: audio, subtitles,
  teletext, RDS, ID3), so the audio plays on from its queue, and the
  clock is neither flushed nor paused;
- the video, once started, is put in sync with that clock as a video
  stream that starts while the audio plays (HandlePlaySpeed(), the branch
  of a video waiting for sync with the audio in sync).

Where that cannot be, the accurate seek of before, and kodi.log says why
(VideoOnlyRefusal()): a DVD, a Blu-ray input, a disc menu, a stream that
cannot seek, a live stream, not at normal speed, buffering, no audio in
sync, the video not in sync yet, a demuxer that is not FFmpeg's own, or
closed captions of the video stream shown; also where the demuxer cannot
go back or goes past the audio already read, and where the video is not
back in sync within 6 s. A seek or a flush ends it.

kodi.log: "the video stream is opened again alone, the audio left
playing: the demuxer went back to N ms for it, its pictures from M ms
are shown...", then "the video is back N ms after its stream was opened
again, in sync with the audio, which played on", or "...opened again with
an accurate seek of the whole player (the sound stops for as long as a
seek): <why>".

Where the output changes (native Dolby Vision to HDR10 or back) the TV
changes its picture mode, and the sound that goes through it may stop
for that time all the same. Test (host): the refusal, the filter and the
times (34 checks; seven mutations caught). Not run on a display.
